        "It's like asking a stranger at a different company to hold a 'maybe' on a payment they haven't actually processed yet, and wait indefinitely for a phone call from your office before deciding whether to actually charge the card or cancel — most outside companies simply aren't set up to do business that way.\n\n• What 2PC requires of every participant: durably log the intended change, vote yes or no during Prepare, then hold that prepared state (and typically its locks) until the coordinator's final Commit or Abort decision arrives — potentially across multiple cross-network messages and real delay.\n\n• Why an external gateway won't do this: it's a separate organisation's system, reached over the open internet through its own API, with no reason to implement USyd's prepare-vote-commit protocol or to trust USyd's coordinator's timing enough to hold a card authorisation open indefinitely.\n\n• The course's own framing: ACID/2PC-style coordination is suitable only \"among closely-coupled, trusting, fast/reliable-communicating systems\" — a third-party payment gateway is the opposite of that on every count: loosely coupled, external, and not obliged to trust anyone else's coordinator.\n\n• Why the other options are wrong: the real issue isn't raw processing speed, matching tech stacks, or any security rule about returning funds during Prepare — it's that 2PC demands a specific prepare-vote-hold protocol and a trust relationship an independent external gateway has no reason to implement.\n\nSo the answer is: because 2PC requires every participant to log, vote, and hold a prepared state until a coordinator's decision arrives, and an external, independently-operated gateway has no reason to implement that protocol or trust USyd's coordinator.",
