        "The Interactive Response Time Law is basically rearranging a simple idea: each full round trip for one terminal is however long it waits for a response, plus however long it then spends 'thinking' before asking again — multiply that by how many terminals are doing this at once, and you get the total time it takes to produce however many completed jobs you actually saw.\n\n• First, find observed throughput: X = completed transactions ÷ observation time = 900 ÷ 1,200 seconds = 0.75 transactions/sec.\n\n• Then apply the law: R = N/X − Z, with N = 10 terminals and Z = 8 seconds think time: R = 10/0.75 − 8 ≈ 13.33 − 8 = 5.33 seconds.\n\n• Sanity-checking against the bounds: the low-load floor is D_app + D_db = 0.6 + 0.8 = 1.4 seconds, and 5.33s sits comfortably above that floor, while the high-load bound hasn't started to bind yet — exactly the behaviour expected in the \"knee\" region, where 10 terminals is close to, but not past, the system's saturation point.\n\n• Why the other options are wrong: multiplying N by X instead of dividing uses the wrong operation entirely and produces a nonsensical negative number; inverting throughput's units (seconds/transaction instead of transactions/second) or multiplying instead of dividing the raw counts both break the formula's units and produce meaningless or negative results that get waved away instead of signalling an error.\n\nSo the answer is: observed response time ≈ 5.33 seconds, consistent with 10 terminals sitting in the knee region just below saturation.",
