        "The high-load bound asks: once the system is completely saturated, how fast can the slowest device possibly go, no matter how many more people pile on? The low-load bound asks the opposite question: with hardly anyone around, how closely does throughput just track the number of people actually using it?\n\n• High-load bound: Xmax ≤ 1/Dmax, using the bottleneck's own service demand (Dmax = 0.8s, the database server from the earlier part) = 1/0.8 = 1.25 transactions/sec, about 75/minute — this is the absolute ceiling no matter how many terminals get added.\n\n• Low-load bound at N = 4: X(N) ≤ N/(D+Z), where D is the total demand across all devices (D_app + D_db = 0.6 + 0.8 = 1.4s) and Z is think time (8s): X(4) ≤ 4/(1.4+8) = 4/9.4 ≈ 0.4255 transactions/sec, about 25.5/minute — roughly what you'd expect overnight with so few terminals active.\n\n• Why the other options are wrong: using the smaller demand (D_min) instead of the bottleneck's own demand (Dmax) for the high-load bound gets the wrong device; omitting D from the low-load bound's denominator, using Dmax directly as a rate, or multiplying instead of dividing all break the actual formulas and produce numbers with the wrong units or an implausible scale.\n\nSo the answer is: high-load bound ≈ 1.25 transactions/sec (the hard ceiling), and low-load bound at N=4 ≈ 0.4255 transactions/sec.",
